          'A 45-year-old female is referred to outpatient PM&R 6 weeks after emergent L4-5 laminectomy and discectomy for cauda equina syndrome. She presented to the ED with acute low back pain radiating bilaterally into her legs, saddle anesthesia, and urinary retention (post-void residual 800 mL). Surgery was performed within 12 hours of symptom onset. She now has residual bilateral lower extremity weakness (left > right), persistent saddle hypoesthesia, neurogenic bladder requiring intermittent catheterization, and neurogenic bowel. She walks with bilateral Lofstrand crutches. She is a 45-year-old married mother of two teenage children and works as a high school physical education teacher. She appears embarrassed and hesitant during the interview, and near the end of the visit, quietly asks: "Doctor, will I ever be able to... be intimate with my husband again? We have not been able to since this happened, and he says it is fine, but I can see it is not."',
