          '**Vitals**: BP 118/72 mmHg, HR 68 bpm, RR 14, SpO2 99% on RA, Temp 36.6C, BMI 24.\n\n**General**: Athletic-appearing female. Walks into clinic with bilateral Lofstrand crutches and left AFO. Appears anxious and avoids eye contact when discussing bladder/bowel/sexual topics.\n\n**Neurological**:\n- **Motor**: **Right**: Hip flexion 4/5, knee extension 4+/5, ankle dorsiflexion 4/5, ankle plantarflexion 4/5, great toe extension 4/5, hip abduction 4/5. **Left**: Hip flexion 3+/5, knee extension 4/5, ankle dorsiflexion 2/5, ankle plantarflexion 3/5, great toe extension 1/5, hip abduction 3/5. Bilateral gluteal atrophy noted.\n- **Sensory**: Saddle hypoesthesia — diminished sensation S2-S5 bilaterally (perineum, perianal area, posterior thighs). Can detect deep pressure but not light touch or pinprick in the saddle region. Diminished sensation L5-S1 distribution left foot.\n- **Reflexes**: Bilateral ankle reflexes absent. Bilateral knee reflexes 1+. Bulbocavernosus reflex absent. Anal wink absent.\n- **Tone**: Flaccid — no spasticity (LMN injury pattern).\n\n**Rectal Exam**: Decreased perianal sensation. Decreased resting anal sphincter tone. No voluntary anal contraction.\n\n**Gait**: Ambulates with bilateral Lofstrand crutches. Left foot drop — compensated by AFO. Trendelenburg sign left (weak hip abductors). Gait endurance limited to approximately 500 feet.\n\n**Musculoskeletal**: Lumbar incision well-healed. Mild lumbar tenderness. Full lumbar ROM except for guarding at end-range flexion. Negative bilateral straight-leg raise.',
