    Section(
      title: '+ Explain the pathophysiology, surgical timing implications, and prognosis for neurogenic bladder and sexual function in cauda equina syndrome.',
      content:
          '**Pathophysiology of Cauda Equina Syndrome (CES)**:\n- The cauda equina consists of the nerve roots L2-S5 descending through the lumbar spinal canal below the conus medullaris (which terminates at approximately L1-L2).\n- **CES results from compression of these peripheral nerve roots**, most commonly by a massive central disc herniation (as in this case), but also by tumor, abscess, hematoma, or spinal stenosis.\n- Because these are **peripheral nerves (LMN)**, the injury pattern differs fundamentally from spinal cord injury (UMN): **flaccid weakness, areflexic bladder and bowel, absent bulbocavernosus reflex, no spasticity**.\n- The sacral nerve roots (S2-S4) are particularly vulnerable due to their central position in the thecal sac — they are compressed first and most severely by central disc herniations. This explains why bladder, bowel, and sexual function are affected disproportionately.\n\n**Surgical Timing and Prognosis**:\n- CES is a **surgical emergency**. The debate centers on the optimal window:\n- **<24 hours**: Traditional teaching. Meta-analyses show improved outcomes compared to >48 hours.\n- **<48 hours**: Some studies show equivalent outcomes to <24 hours, but most experts recommend surgery as soon as possible.\n- **This patient**: Surgery at 12 hours — within the optimal window, but she presented with **CES with retention (CES-R)** — the most severe form, which carries a worse prognosis than CES with incomplete symptoms.\n\n**Bladder Recovery Prognosis**:\n- Bladder function recovers in approximately **40-70% of CES patients** operated on within 24-48 hours.\n- CES-R (this patient\'s presentation) has **worse bladder recovery** than CES-incomplete (30-50% recovery rate).\n- Recovery can continue for **up to 2-3 years** as peripheral nerves regenerate (approximately 1 mm/day).\n- **Unfavorable prognostic signs**: Absent bulbocavernosus reflex at 6 weeks (this patient), bilateral saddle anesthesia, absent anal contraction.\n- **This patient**: At 6 weeks post-surgery, still requiring CIC with no sensation of bladder fullness — likely to have permanent neurogenic bladder, though some improvement may still occur.\n\n**Sexual Function Prognosis**:\n- S2-S4 nerve roots mediate genital sensation (pudendal nerve), clitoral engorgement (parasympathetic), vaginal lubrication, and orgasm.\n- **Women with CES**: Loss of genital sensation is the primary sexual complaint. Orgasmic dysfunction is common. Vaginal dryness due to impaired parasympathetic innervation.\n- Recovery parallels bladder recovery — if S2-S4 roots recover, sexual sensation and function may improve.\n- Unlike male sexual dysfunction post-CES (where PDE5 inhibitors can assist with erectile function), **female sexual dysfunction after CES has fewer pharmacologic treatment options**, making counseling and adaptive strategies particularly important.\n- Prognosis for this patient: Guarded. Some return of sensation is possible over 1-2 years but complete recovery is unlikely given the severity of initial presentation.',
