    Section(
      title: '+ Outline a rehabilitation plan for cauda equina syndrome with neurogenic bladder and bowel, including vocational considerations.',
      content:
          '**Comprehensive Rehabilitation Plan**:\n\n**1. Lower Extremity Strengthening and Gait Training**:\n- **Progressive strengthening**: Peripheral nerve recovery may continue for 18-24 months. Focus on weak muscle groups — left ankle dorsiflexion (2/5), left great toe extension (1/5), bilateral hip abductors and gluteals.\n- **Electrical stimulation**: NMES to left tibialis anterior and peroneal muscles to maintain muscle bulk and facilitate nerve recovery.\n- **AFO optimization**: Current left AFO for foot drop. As dorsiflexion recovers, may transition to a less rigid design or discontinue.\n- **Gait retraining**: Progress from bilateral Lofstrand crutches to single crutch, then cane, then independent as strength allows. Address left Trendelenburg with hip abductor strengthening and gait cueing.\n- **Endurance training**: Stationary cycling (low resistance), aquatic therapy, progressive walking program.\n- **Balance training**: Single-leg stance, dynamic balance activities, perturbation training.\n\n**2. Neurogenic Bladder Management**:\n- **Continue CIC every 4-6 hours**: Teach optimal technique — clean (not sterile) technique, adequate lubrication, comfortable positioning. She must become fully independent and efficient.\n- **Urodynamics**: Order at 3 months post-injury to characterize bladder dynamics — capacity, compliance, detrusor function, leak point pressure. This guides long-term management.\n- **Monitor renal function**: Annual renal ultrasound to screen for hydronephrosis. Annual serum creatinine.\n- **UTI prevention**: Adequate hydration (2L/day). Proper CIC technique. Do NOT use prophylactic antibiotics routinely. Treat symptomatic UTIs only (not asymptomatic bacteriuria, which is common with CIC).\n- **Medications**: Continue tamsulosin (reduces urethral resistance, facilitating drainage). If urodynamics show poor compliance, consider anticholinergics cautiously.\n- **Workplace considerations**: She needs private bathroom access and scheduled breaks for CIC as a PE teacher.\n\n**3. Neurogenic Bowel Management**:\n- **LMN bowel pattern**: Areflexic, flaccid external sphincter. Risk of passive stool leakage rather than constipation.\n- **Program**: Scheduled manual evacuation every other day at the same time. Valsalva maneuver or abdominal massage to assist. Maintain stool consistency with fiber and stool softeners.\n- **Diet**: High fiber (25-30 g/day) with adequate water. Avoid gas-producing foods.\n- **Continence strategies**: Predictable evacuation schedule to prevent accidents. May need incontinence pad as backup for PE teaching (physically active, cannot always get to bathroom quickly).\n\n**4. Pain Management**:\n- Gabapentin 300 mg TID — optimize if neuropathic pain persists. Consider pregabalin for better tolerability.\n- Core stabilization exercises for residual low back pain.\n- Avoid heavy lifting permanently — re-herniation risk.\n\n**5. Return to Work as PE Teacher**:\n- **Functional capacity evaluation**: At 3-4 months, assess her ability to demonstrate exercises, supervise activities, manage a gym/field environment.\n- **Accommodations needed**: Access to private bathroom for CIC (scheduled breaks), modified duty initially (no heavy lifting or demonstration of high-impact activities), possible aide assistance.\n- **Gradual return**: Part-time schedule initially, progressing to full-time.\n- **ADA protections**: Her condition qualifies as a disability under the ADA. Employer must provide reasonable accommodations.\n- **Job modification**: May need to shift from full physical demonstration to coaching/instruction model. Can use student demonstrators for exercises she cannot perform.',
